Cthulhu: drop CFBundleIconName so our firefox.icns is what macOS shows.

Upstream sets BOTH icon keys. CFBundleIconName names an "AppIcon" asset
inside Contents/Resources/Assets.car, and LaunchServices prefers it over
CFBundleIconFile -- so the Dock kept drawing Firefox's gradient sphere
even though firefox.icns had been replaced with our pixel art. Measured
with NSWorkspace.icon(forFile:): 4709 distinct colours at 128px, against
the 16 our art actually contains.

Assets.car is a compiled asset catalog produced from Mozilla's own
artwork and shipped by browser/app/Makefile.in; regenerating it would
mean carrying an .xcassets and an actool (full Xcode) build step purely
to restate an icon we already ship as .icns. Removing the key makes
macOS fall back to CFBundleIconFile, which is the ordinary path and
works on every version. The stale Assets.car stays in the bundle but is
no longer consulted for the app icon.

diff --git a/browser/app/macbuild/Contents/Info.plist.in b/browser/app/macbuild/Contents/Info.plist.in
index c45e7f9b70..2795b177f4 100644
--- a/browser/app/macbuild/Contents/Info.plist.in
+++ b/browser/app/macbuild/Contents/Info.plist.in
@@ -190,8 +190,6 @@
 	<string>@MAC_APP_NAME@ @APP_VERSION@</string>
 	<key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
 	<string>firefox.icns</string>
-	<key>CFBundleIconName</key>
-	<string>AppIcon</string>
 	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
 	<string>@MOZ_MACBUNDLE_ID@</string>
 	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
